<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec3-critically-damped-crossing" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analyzing a Critically Damped System: Finding Maximum Stretch</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SETUP: ODE AND SOLUTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Consider the spring-mass-dashpot system with position function
    <me>x(t) = -9e^{-t} + 3te^{-t}, \quad t \geq 0.</me>
    As usual, <m>x(t) > 0</m> means the spring is stretched and <m>x(t) \lt 0</m>
    means it is compressed.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-critically-damped-crossing">
    <title>Finding the Time of Maximum Stretch</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>x(t) = -9e^{-t} + 3te^{-t}</m>, find the time at which the
        spring is maximally stretched, or explain why the spring is never stretched.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Work through the following steps:
        <ol>
          <li><p>Find <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m>. Does the mass start compressed or stretched?</p></li>
          <li><p>Solve <m>x(t) = 0</m> for <m>t > 0</m>. Does the mass ever become stretched by crossing the equilibrium?</p></li>
          <li><p>Since the mass starts compressed but does become stretched, the maximum stretch cannot occur at <m>t = 0</m>. Where must it occur?</p></li>
          <li><p>Solve <m>x'(t) = 0</m> to find the exact time.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </hint>

    <solution>

      <p>
        <alert>Step 1:</alert> The mass starts <alert>compressed</alert>.
      </p>
      <solution>
        <title>Work for Step 1</title>
        <p>
          We compute <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m>:
          <me>x(0) = -9e^0 + 3(0)e^0 = -9 \lt 0,</me>
          so the mass starts compressed. Differentiating using the product rule:
          <me>x'(t) = -(-9e^{-t}) + 3e^{-t} + 3t(-e^{-t}) = 9e^{-t} + 3e^{-t} - 3te^{-t} = e^{-t}(12 - 3t),</me>
          so <m>x'(0) = e^0(12) = 12 > 0</m>. The mass starts compressed and is
          initially moving in the positive direction, toward the equilibrium.
        </p>
      </solution>

      <p>
        <alert>Step 2:</alert> The mass crosses the equilibrium at <alert><m>t = 3</m></alert>,
        so it does eventually become stretched.
      </p>
      <solution>
        <title>Work for Step 2</title>
        <p>
          We solve <m>x(t) = 0</m>:
          <me>-9e^{-t} + 3te^{-t} = 0 \implies e^{-t}(-9 + 3t) = 0.</me>
          Since <m>e^{-t} \neq 0</m>, we need <m>-9 + 3t = 0</m>, giving <m>t = 3</m>.
          Since <m>t = 3 > 0</m>, this is a valid crossing time. The mass crosses
          the equilibrium exactly once, at <m>t = 3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>

      <p>
        <alert>Step 3:</alert> The maximum stretch occurs at a <alert>critical point</alert> where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <solution>
        <title>Reasoning for Step 3</title>
        <p>
          The maximum stretch occurs either at <m>t = 0</m> or at a critical point
          where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>. Since the mass starts compressed (<m>x(0) = -9 \lt 0</m>),
          the maximum stretch cannot occur at <m>t = 0</m>. The mass crosses the
          equilibrium at <m>t = 3</m> and becomes stretched, so the maximum stretch
          must occur at some <m>t > 3</m>. Since it is a local maximum at a valid
          time, it must be a critical point.
        </p>
      </solution>

      <p>
        <alert>Step 4:</alert> The maximum stretch occurs at <alert><m>t = 4</m></alert>.
      </p>
      <solution>
        <title>Work for Step 4</title>
        <p>
          We solve <m>x'(t) = 0</m>. From Step 1 we have
          <m>x'(t) = e^{-t}(12 - 3t)</m>.
          Since <m>e^{-t} \neq 0</m>:
          <me>12 - 3t = 0 \implies t = 4.</me>
          So the maximum stretch occurs at <m>t = 4</m>, where
          <me>x(4) = -9e^{-4} + 3(4)e^{-4} = e^{-4}(-9 + 12) = 3e^{-4} \approx 0.055.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>

    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE SUMMARY + GRAPH
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Notice that we have determined the following about the motion:
    <ul>
      <li><p>The mass starts <alert>compressed</alert> (since <m>x(0) = -9 \lt 0</m>).</p></li>
      <li><p>The mass crosses the equilibrium at <alert><m>t = 3</m></alert> (the only solution to <m>x(t) = 0</m> for <m>t > 0</m>).</p></li>
      <li><p>Maximum stretch occurs at <alert><m>t = 4</m></alert> (occurs at the only critical point where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>).</p></li>
      <li><p>After <m>t = 4</m>, the mass never crosses the equilibrium again, but we know <m>\displaystyle\lim_{t \to \infty} x(t) = 0</m>.</p></li>
    </ul>
    Now that you have had a lot of practice building pictures of motion,
    try sketching the graph yourself using the information above before
    answering the checkpoint below.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <task xml:id="task-critically-damped-crossing-mc">
    <title>Behavior After Crossing Equilibrium</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Based on the graph and the work above, which best describes what
        the mass does after crossing the equilibrium at <m>t = 3</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The mass becomes more and more stretched until it reaches maximum
            stretch at <m>t = 4</m>, then gradually becomes less stretched as
            it slowly approaches the equilibrium, never crossing it again.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! After crossing at <m>t = 3</m> the mass is on the stretched
            side. It continues stretching until the critical point at <m>t = 4</m>,
            then slowly decays back toward the equilibrium. Since
            <m>\lim_{t \to \infty} x(t) = 0</m> and the mass never crosses
            equilibrium again, it approaches but never reaches it again. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The mass immediately starts returning to equilibrium after crossing,
            reaching it again shortly after <m>t = 3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. After crossing at <m>t = 3</m>, the mass continues moving
            in the positive direction (it is still stretching) until the critical
            point at <m>t = 4</m>. It does not immediately turn around.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The mass oscillates above and below the equilibrium multiple times
            before settling at zero.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is the behavior of an underdamped system. Since
            <m>x(t) = -9e^{-t} + 3te^{-t}</m> is critically damped, it crosses
            the equilibrium at most once and does not oscillate.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </task>

